  "query_preview": "..(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 215 PM EDT SAT OCT 24 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, a stubborn 500 mb ridge \nwill still be centered just west of the forecast area, with a well\ndefined negatively tilted shortwave trough over the Central \nPlains, just west of this ridge. At the surface, this shortwave \nwill correspond to an area of low pressure near the KS/CO border, \nwith surface high pressure firmly in place over the Ohio Valley. \nAs this shortwave lifts northeast along the periphery of the \nridge, it will work to flatten the ridge somewhat, and a second \nmidlevel disturbance will also work to reinforce troughing over \nthe Northern Rockies and the Pacific Northwest. Eventually, these \nimpulses will finally break down the ridge over our area. \nMeanwhile, at the surface, an area of low pressure associated with\nthe first shortwave will lift to the northeast from the Plains \ninto the Upper Midwest. A diffuse frontal boundary, previously \nstalled over Ohio, will also lift back north of the area with this\nsystem as a warm front, completely failing to affect our local \nweather. Instead, high pressure will continue to dominate through \nthe end of the work week. \n\nWith high pressure in control, extremely pleasant weather is \nexpected to continue through Friday. Highs each day will be above \naverage, but still only rising into the low to mid 70s,\naccompanied by ample sunshine. A possible exception could be \nWednesday, when a shift to southwesterly flow aloft will allow for\nan embedded shortwave disturbance or two to pass over the area. \nEven so, in the absence of better moisture, no rainfall is \nexpected. Instead, the main impact to the forecast would be \nincreased cloud cover on Wednesday, with marginally warmer lows \npossible should cloud cover persist overnight. Otherwise, high \npressure and a weak pressure gradient will allow for ridge valley\ntemperature splits to develop, with lows in the valleys generally\nranging from the upper 30s to low 40s in the first portion of the\nperiod, while ridges remain more in the low to mid 40s. Expect \ntypical fog formation to accompany these cool temperatures in \nmainstem river valleys. Patchy frost cannot be ruled out for the \ncoolest and most sheltered valleys early Tuesday morning; however,\nthis will largely depend on dewpoints going into the night, which\nwill influence the magnitude of cooling in these locations. Raw \nNBM dewpoints are almost certainly too moist, an observation \ncorroborated by ensemble guidance. LREF mean dewpoints look to be \nin the low to mid 40s rather than the NBM's low 50s, and given the\ndepicted dry layer in deterministic model soundings, in addition \nto a lack of truly appreciable moisture advection, these 40s \ndewpoints seem reasonable. Even so, it is worth noting that should\ndewpoints be drier than forecast, more significant cooling would \nbe possible, increasing the chance for patchy frost in hollows \nwithout a body of water. Regardless, minimum temperatures do trend\nwarmer through the period, which should keep frost risk lower \nlater in the week, although valleys will still be cooler than \nridgetops. \n\nBy the end of the period, model spread increases significantly\nwith respect to whether a ridging or troughing pattern is favored\nlocally. However, it appears clear that rain is unlikely through\nSaturday, with the majority of LREF members favoring no measurable\nprecipitation through the entire long term period. In short,\nweather should remain pleasant.",
